  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nPersistent radar echoes moving in from the west since early this\nmorning have only resulted in very light amounts, save for a\ntenth or so near London Mills (Fulton County). The rain has been\nbattling low level dry air advection from the east, as well as a\nnotable dry layer aloft between about 850 and 700 mb. Points\nwest of the Illinois River have managed to moisten that dry\nlayer the most and stand the best chance for a few more hours of\noccasional sprinkles or light rain through this evening.\n\nSome locations could see some very light precipitation this \nweekend in association with a north-south deformation axis, \nbeginning on Saturday primarily west of I-55 and gradually\nmoving to roughly a Bloomington-Effingham line early Sunday\nmorning. Otherwise, the elongated blocking ridge over the\nMidwest between a western US trough and a closed low over the\nmid-Atlantic should keep the weather low-impact and relatively\ndry.\n\nThat is forecast to change around the middle of next week, as\nthe trough over the western US begins to move toward the central\npart of the country, potentially picking up tropical moisture\nfrom now-Hurricane Polo as it does so. A slow-moving boundary\nwith perhaps multiple short-wave troughs tracking along it \ncould be a recipe for heavy rain setting up somewhere in the \ngeneral vicinity. Ensemble solutions are split between a group \nthat features a deeper closed low originating over the southwest\nUS, and a group that features a stronger northern stream \ntrough. The northern stream group has a higher average \nprecipitation amount for central Illinois, but either way there \nis a 20-50% chance of greater than two inches of precipitation \nby the end of next weekend, with the higher probabilities west \nof the Illinois River.",
